\section{Null models}{

With \code{n_perm > 0}, \code{method = "base"} compares each statistic with a null distribution, from
the three null models of Taudiere & Violle (2016), implemented in base R from their
description:
\itemize{
\item \code{T_IP.IC}: trait values are permuted among the individuals of each site, which keeps the
variance of the site and breaks the structure of populations within it;
\item \code{T_IC.IR}: trait values are permuted among all individuals of the region, so that each
site receives as many values as it has individuals, drawn without replacement from the
region (their null model \code{regional.ind});
\item \code{T_PC.PR}: each individual takes the mean of its population, these values are permuted
among all individuals of the region, and population means are computed again from the
values each population received (their null model \code{regional.pop}, \code{null_pc = "individuals"}); by default (\code{null_pc = "populations"}), population means are permuted
among the populations of the region instead (see the section Null model of T_PC.PR and the
size of populations).
}

Each randomisation is one permutation of the whole region, so the sites of a randomisation
share the regional pool without repeating its individuals, as the sites of the data do. The
null distribution of each site is that of a draw without replacement from the region, and
the null distribution of the mean over sites, tested by \code{\link[=tstats_summary]{tstats_summary()}}, keeps the
dependence among sites. Up to version 0.10.0 (and in \code{\link[cati:Tstats]{cati::Tstats()}}) each site drew from
the region on its own, and individuals were repeated across the sites of a randomisation:
the test of each site of \code{T_IC.IR} had the same null distribution, but the test of the mean
at 5\% rejected in 0 of 600 data sets for \code{T_IC.IR} and in 7.8\% for \code{T_PC.PR} when
individuals were placed at random (8 sites, 199 randomisations); with one permutation of
the region both rates are compatible with 5\%.

The denominator of \code{T_IC.IR} (the variance among all individuals) is not changed by the
permutation. In the null model of \code{T_PC.PR} the population means are themselves computed
from the values drawn, so their regional variance is computed again from them, as in
Taudiere & Violle (2016).

The standardised effect size is \eqn{(T_{obs} - \bar T_{null}) / s_{null}}{(T_obs -
mean(T_null)) / sd(T_null)}, and the p-value is \eqn{(1 + r) / (1 + n)}{(1 + r) / (1 + n)},
with \eqn{n} = \code{n_perm}, where \eqn{r} is the number of null values at or below the observed
one (\code{alternative = "less"}), at or above it (\code{"greater"}), or twice the smaller of the two
(\code{"two.sided"}, at most 1). Low values of the three statistics are the usual hypotheses
(niche differentiation within communities for \code{T_IP.IC}, environmental filtering for the
others), so \code{"less"} tests them. Each trait is randomised on its own.

The null model of \code{T_IP.IC} asks only whether populations differ: permuting individuals
within a site removes its populations, so null values are near 1, and co-occurring species
with different means give a large negative effect size even when each population sits where
its species would be at any site. With \code{null_ip = "species"}, the mean of each
population is instead exchanged at random with that of another population of the same
species, and its individuals keep their number and their deviations from the mean. Each site
keeps its species, their abundances and the variances within populations; only which mean of
a species sits at which site is broken. Moving whole populations instead would also break
the match between the abundance of a species at a site and its trait, which environmental
filtering of abundances creates, and would test that instead. The standardised effect size is near zero when the position of a population does not depend on
the site, positive when co-occurring populations are closer than expected from their species
(convergence, as under local adaptation to a shared environment), and negative when they are
further apart (divergence, as under character displacement); \code{alternative = "two.sided"}
tests both. Species found at a single site keep their mean. Means of populations with
different numbers of individuals carry different sampling error and are not fully
exchangeable, which makes the test conservative (effect sizes shrink toward zero and a test
at 5\% rejects less often than 5\% when nothing acts) but not anti-conservative. Local adaptation and character
displacement move this statistic in opposite directions and can cancel; see
\code{\link[=population_shifts]{population_shifts()}} to estimate both.

\code{method = "cati"} calls \code{\link[cati:Tstats]{cati::Tstats()}} (Taudiere & Violle 2016) instead and returns its
object. Both methods give the same observed values. Two problems of cati 0.99.6 affect it:
\code{\link[cati:Tstats]{cati::Tstats()}} finds the populations of a site by matching its name as a regular
expression, so a site called \code{site1} would also take the populations of \code{site10}
(\url{https://github.com/adrientaudiere/cati/issues/11}); the sites are therefore passed
under labels of equal width (\code{site01}, \code{site02}, ...) and given back their names in the
result. And its null model for \code{T_IP.IC} puts together the permutations of different sites
in the wrong order, so that the null values of a site come from the individuals of other
sites; its standardised effect sizes for \code{T_IP.IC} are then wrong
(\url{https://github.com/adrientaudiere/cati/issues/12}). \code{method = "base"} is recommended.
}

\section{Null model of T_PC.PR and the size of populations}{

With \code{null_pc = "individuals"} a null population mean is the mean of as many values as the
population has individuals, so the null model is that of individuals placed at random among
species and sites, and the spread of the null means depends on the size of the populations:
populations of one or two individuals vary widely and large ones stay near the regional
mean. Two consequences follow when species differ in their means. A site with large, even
populations gets null values well below those of sites with small populations, and the
other way round, whatever the trait values: with species placed at random among sites, a
test at 5\% for high values at a site of six populations of 10 individuals rejected in about
80\% of 200 data sets when other sites had populations of 1 to 45 individuals, and the test
for low values at these sites in 8\%. And the test asks whether individuals are exchangeable,
not whether the species of a site are a random draw of the species of the region.

With \code{null_pc = "populations"} the observed population means are instead permuted among the
populations of the region, one value per population (the null model \code{regional.pop.prab} of
Taudiere & Violle 2016, which they do not use for T-statistics). Each site keeps its number
of populations, the regional variance of the means is that of the data, and the size of
populations plays no part; in the example above both tests rejected in 1 to 5\%. It is the
default, because the sizes of populations usually differ among sites in field data;
\code{null_pc = "individuals"} gives the null model of Taudiere & Violle (2016), the default up
to version 0.10.0. Populations of the same species at different sites have similar means,
which this permutation treats as independent, so the test tends to be conservative when
species differ and are placed at random.

Whatever the null model, \code{T_PC.PR} is a ratio of variances among population means that are
not weighted by abundance, and the test of one site has little power when the site has few
populations: the variance among three means is so variable that, for normally distributed
means, 5\% of the null values of \code{null_pc = "populations"} fall below about 0.05 (0.18 with
five populations). The test of the mean over sites of \code{\link[=tstats_summary]{tstats_summary()}} has more power.
}

\section{Local adaptation and character displacement}{

With \code{local_adaptation} in \code{\link[=sample_individuals]{sample_individuals()}}, population means move toward the
environment of their site, so co-occurring species become more alike and the region spreads
along the environment. The variance among populations within a site falls, so \code{T_PC.PR} and
\code{T_IC.IR} fall, and \code{T_IP.IC} rises, because the populations that remain overlap more. With
\code{displacement}, co-occurring populations diverge from one another instead, which raises the
variance among populations within sites and lowers \code{T_IP.IC}.
}

